First Author: Pelzer
Title: The Accuracy of Initial U.S. Heart Transplant Candidate Rankings
Source number: 40  ￼

Very short summary: This SRTR registry study compared the current U.S. 6-status heart allocation ranking with multivariable urgency prediction models for death before transplant. Five key findings were: (1) 32,294 adult heart-transplant candidates were analyzed; (2) the 6-status system had only moderate discrimination at listing, with C-index 0.67; (3) Cox proportional hazards and random survival forest models trained on prepolicy data performed better in the postpolicy cohort, with C-indices 0.76 and 0.74; (4) statuses 4 and 6 had no significant survival difference, while status 5 had worse survival than status 4; and (5) physiologic measurements, especially glomerular filtration rate, had the highest variable importance. Tables 1–2, Figure 1, and the Kaplan–Meier/calibration figures were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

